          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What follow-up interval does guidance describe after starting a new blood pressure medicine?",
          "answer": "After starting or changing a blood pressure medication, guidance from major cardiology societies recommends reassessment at roughly monthly intervals until the target blood pressure is reached. Once readings are stable, reviews can occur every three to six months.\n\nSpecifically, for agents acting on the renin-angiotensin system, monthly review until the target is reached should also include checking kidney function and potassium levels. (Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets; Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line)\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
